---
import Card from "astro-theme-university/components/Card.astro";
import CardGrid from "astro-theme-university/components/CardGrid.astro";
import ContentLayout from "astro-theme-university/layouts/ContentLayout.astro";
import heroImage from "../assets/images/hero-home.avif";
import { courseMeta } from "../course-config";
import { sessionLabels, siteConfig } from "../site-config";
---

<ContentLayout
  title={`${courseMeta.code}: ${courseMeta.title}`}
  description={courseMeta.description}
  heroImage={heroImage}
  heroImageAlt="A stack of pointer boxes and arrows in flat gold and black, one arrow crossing a broken boundary line, in a two-ink risograph print"
  {...siteConfig}
>
  <ul class="course-tags" aria-label="Course tags">
    {courseMeta.tags.map((tag) => <li>{tag}</li>)}
  </ul>

  <h2>What you will do</h2>
  <p>
    Twelve weeks, one retrofit a week: Cyclone, Checked C, separation logic,
    Frama-C, CompCert, MISRA-C and static analysis, and the academic
    borrow-checking prototypes chasing Rust's guarantee inside plain C. You'll
    annotate and verify real (small) C functions with at least three of these
    tools across the semester, run a real CVE through several of them side by
    side, and finish with a report that argues — on your own evidence, not on
    vibes — which tool you'd actually ship with.
  </p>

  <h2>Who it is for</h2>
  <p>
    You should already be comfortable reading and writing C, and unbothered by
    a little formal notation — you don't need prior exposure to Hoare logic or
    separation logic, but week 6 assumes you can follow a proof once it's
    explained, not that you've never seen a `∀`. If "C has no memory safety" is
    news to you, start with week 1's reading list before you enrol.
  </p>

  <h2>Where to go next</h2>
  <CardGrid columns={2}>
    <Card title={sessionLabels.plural} href="/sessions/">
      <p>The weekly schedule and what happens in each teaching session.</p>
    </Card>
    <Card title="Assessment" href="/assessments/">
      <p>What is marked, what it is worth, and when it is due.</p>
    </Card>
    <Card title="People" href="/people/">
      <p>Who teaches the course and how to reach them.</p>
    </Card>
  </CardGrid>
</ContentLayout>

<style>
  .course-tags {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: 0.5rem;
    margin-block: 0 2rem;
    padding: 0;
    list-style: none;
  }

  .course-tags li {
    padding: 0.25rem 0.65rem;
    border-radius: 999px;
    color: var(--at-text-secondary);
    background: var(--at-bg-alt);
    font-size: 0.9rem;
  }
</style>
